 "title": "Algebraic patterns",
 "brief": "On the left, two interlocking wheels of the Engine turn slowly against each other, each a disc ringed with small teeth, one in the accent and one in ink. On the right, a loom: a row of fine vertical warp threads, and across them a woven cloth that ripples gently, its folds traveling across like a pattern being worked. Between them a shuttle slides back and forth along the threads, and a small bead follows it on a spring, a beat behind: the Engine does only what it is told, and the pattern follows from the instruction.\n\nLovelace's line is the whole image: the Analytical Engine weaves algebraical patterns just as the Jacquard loom weaves flowers and leaves. The cloth's ripples and the wheels' turning share one slow rhythm. A field of faint punched dots floats behind the loom like a card held up to the light. No numbers, no labels: the order is in the motion.",
